#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134608} :activity/id #uuid "25739194-82c1-5788-9ee1-b6fee61cbd79" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049399 :topic/id #uuid "f1aa8ae2-23cf-5d91-8af0-a43e9db5e668" :topic/math-academy-id 2214} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135191 :tutorial/content "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nEvery arithmetic sequence can also be written using the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\na\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we're given the following explicit formula for an arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\na_n =2n+6, \\quad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo convert to a recursive formula, we just need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2(1) + 6 = 8 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2(2) + 6 = 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 10 - 8 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}2}, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}8}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6cd14c7-922b-5e05-9c8e-b842717155ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8910 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02ae2395-79da-5dc3-9f17-a75936abdc07" :step/math-academy-id 191930 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134609}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133531 :question/id #uuid "00113d76-b950-513e-a3ea-29eef91c9cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-14383"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b8b10a8-f81a-5dae-b54e-b10959e3ad14" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134446}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula"} :step/id #uuid "ed82570d-8cc5-564d-8da6-d86c3395fced" :step/math-academy-id 191931 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133532 :question/id #uuid "e04ad3e0-888e-51c0-b60c-e816b7df514b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14386"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d36a3a61-e23b-5ce3-ac65-f7ac1691ec4e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134447}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "340d50dc-babe-5074-be5e-52e39f437e88" :step/math-academy-id 191934 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135192 :tutorial/content "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the arithmetic sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}1},\\, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}6},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nand we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1} + 1 \\\\\n&= 6 + 1 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 7 - 6 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute\n\n$$\na_1 = {\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}1}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] &= {\\color{blue}6} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}1}) \\\\[3pt] & =6 + n-1 \\\\[3pt] &=n+5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=n+5, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c5b9f8-4c71-5c1f-8841-3f22646e5074" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8914 :tutorial/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7cd53e1d-4c0c-570b-bef4-68f8081c2fef" :step/math-academy-id 191938 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133533 :question/id #uuid "d732cfb5-9db5-535d-adb1-5d3292f1c497" :question/math-academy-id "e-14389"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e1e439-ec4a-5577-80d5-3b113bf36e74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233578}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "2a238f59-a780-5248-bfa5-a217bec91d6c" :step/math-academy-id 191939 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133534 :question/id #uuid "26b3b89d-d485-51ac-b964-8377ce3aff28" :question/math-academy-id "e-14393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baac268a-629e-5887-885a-49393c72776f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134479}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "c81f0b63-4b36-56b0-bc0f-1535d35453ef" :step/math-academy-id 191943}] :activity/title "Translating Between Explicit and Recursive Formulas for Arithmetic Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134049}]}
